  "content": {
    "id": "v2-47999-p62-practice-slot-10-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": null,
    "prompt": "Show that $P(x)=x^3-2x^2+4x+5$ does not have an integer zero by testing all the possible integer zeros.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "$P(-5)=-190$, $P(-1)=-2$, $P(1)=8$, $P(5)=100$. None is zero, so there is no integer zero.",
      "worked": "Any integer zero must be a factor of $5$, so test $-5,-1,1,5$.\n$$\\begin{aligned}P(-5)&=(-5)^3-2(-5)^2+4(-5)+5=-190\\\\P(-1)&=(-1)^3-2(-1)^2+4(-1)+5=-2\\\\P(1)&=1^3-2(1)^2+4(1)+5=8\\\\P(5)&=5^3-2(5)^2+4(5)+5=100\\end{aligned}$$\nNone of the possible integer zeros gives $P(x)=0$. Therefore $P(x)$ has no integer zero.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 50
  },
